rdia, dyspnea, confusion, nausea, vomiting, and seizures [Data: Relationships (10990, 10988)]. This reaction stems from inhibited acetaldehyde metabolism, causing toxic accumulation. Both '头孢类抗菌药' and '头孢类抗生素' are explicitly tied to this reaction in multiple data sources, highlighting its clinical significance [Data: Relationships (10990, 10988)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Huo Xiang Zheng Qi Shui contains high ethanol levels, creating intrinsic interaction risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Huo Xiang Zheng Qi Shui contains 40–50% pharmaceutical ethanol, which is metabolized to acetaldehyde in the body. When taken with cephalosporins—which block acetaldehyde dehydrogenase—this leads to ethanol or acetaldehyde accumulation, resulting in poisoning, acute liver damage, respiratory arrest, or death [Data: Relationships (16073, 10990, 10904, 10908)]. Pharmacists explicitly warn patients not to combine this preparation with cephalosporins, metronidazole, tinidazole, ketoconazole, or furazolidone [Data: Relationships (16080)]. Thus, despite being an over-the-counter traditional remedy, its alcohol content poses serious pharmacological risks.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Acute liver damage is a documented severe consequence of drug–alcohol interactions\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Acute liver injury (急性肝损害) is cited as a potential severe outcome of combining alcohol-containing products like Huo Xiang Zheng Qi Shui with cephalosporins. The mechanism involves ethanol metabolism disruption leading to hepatotoxicity [Data: Relationships (10990, 10908, 16073)]. While acetaminophen overdose is also mentioned in the same context, the specific linkage between cephalosporins, ethanol accumulation, and liver damage is directly asserted in multiple relationship records [Data: Relationships (10908, 10990)]. This elevates the clinical severity of these interactions beyond transient discomfort to organ failure risk.\"\n        